  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2) / 2, drawn at half height over x, z in [-2, 2]. It is steeper along x, so steps overshoot there once the rate is above 0.4 and the swings grow once it is above 0.8 (1 - 2.5 x rate is then below -1).",
    "Step = -rate x (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, with standard deviation 1.5 x sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)), as when sampling b of 64 points. It is exactly 0 at b = 64.",
    "The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step and the grey arrow the noise-free full-data step, both drawn at true length (scale 1). With noise, a single step can raise the loss; it lowers it only on average.",
    "A run takes 5 steps a second while playing and ends at step 40. It is marked diverged when the ball leaves the drawn bowl or the loss exceeds 10; the ball is then drawn at the bowl's rim, and the loss readout keeps its true value.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed. A new run continues the noise sequence, so its noise differs. Reset replays the sequence from the start and clears the ghost path.",
    "If you change a setting during a run, the following steps use the new value."
  ]
}